{
  "name": "two-instances-never-merge",
  "acceptance_item": 1,
  "note": "radarr and sonarr both run in one compose project, both speak Servarr API v3, and both name their third indexer `indexer:3`. The minted id string is identical and the scope is what keeps them apart \u2014 so a collator keying on the native name alone publishes ONE object whose facts are whichever instance answered last, and nothing anywhere says a reading was lost. The third round is the half that makes the scope real rather than decorative: radarr re-commits with nothing, which retires radarr's row and must leave sonarr's untouched. A collator that scoped ids but not RETIREMENT passes the first two rounds and deletes another instance's objects on the third.",
